{"entryType":"UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)","primaryAccession":"P04977","secondaryAccessions":["O69258","O70057","Q599G4","Q5EIB9","Q93V22"],"uniProtkbId":"TOX1_BORPE","entryAudit":{"firstPublicDate":"1987-08-13","lastAnnotationUpdateDate":"2026-09-02","lastSequenceUpdateDate":"1987-08-13","entryVersion":155,"sequenceVersion":1},"annotationScore":5.0,"organism":{"scientificName":"Bordetella pertussis (strain Tohama I / ATCC BAA-589 / NCTC 13251)","taxonId":257313,"lineage":["Bacteria","Pseudomonadati","Pseudomonadota","Betaproteobacteria","Burkholderiales","Alcaligenaceae","Bordetella"]},"proteinExistence":"1: Evidence at protein level","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"value":"Pertussis toxin subunit 1"},"shortNames":[{"value":"PTX S1"}]},"alternativeNames":[{"fullName":{"value":"Islet-activating protein S1"},"shortNames":[{"value":"IAP S1"}]},{"fullName":{"value":"NAD-dependent ADP-ribosyltransferase"},"ecNumbers":[{"value":"2.4.2.-"}]}],"flag":"Precursor"},"genes":[{"geneName":{"value":"ptxA"},"orderedLocusNames":[{"value":"BP3783"}]}],"comments":[{"texts":[{"value":"S1 is an NAD-dependent ADP-ribosyltransferase, which plays a crucial role in the pathogenesis of B.pertussis causing disruption of normal host cellular regulation. It catalyzes the ADP-ribosylation of a cysteine in the alpha subunit of host heterotrimeric G proteins. In the absence of G proteins it also catalyzes the cleavage of NAD(+) into ADP-ribose and nicotinamide. It irreversibly uncouples the G-alpha GTP-binding proteins from their membrane receptors"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"8637000"}],"value":"Pertussis toxin contains five different chains, S1-S5. They are organized into 2 functional subunits: A, composed of S1 (which is toxic) and B, containing S2, S3, S5, and two copies of S4 (B binds to the membrane receptors). Dimers of S2-S4 and S3-S4 are held together by S5"}],"commentType":"SUBUNIT"},{"commentType":"SUBCELLULAR LOCATION","note":{"texts":[{"value":"The individual chains are secreted by a sec-dependent mechanism into the periplasm. Then, S1 associates with the outer membrane before it joins with the B subunit to form the secretion-competent holotoxin. The type IV secretion system ptl mediates secretion of assembled toxin through the outer membrane"}]},"subcellularLocations":[{"location":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"11854200"}],"value":"Secreted","id":"SL-0243"}}]},{"texts":[{"value":"Activated by the two-component regulatory system BvgS/BvgA"}],"commentType":"INDUCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"2455296"}],"value":"The region which spans amino acids 42-49 is required for enzymatic activity and contributes to the formation of a potentially important antigenic determinant"}],"commentType":"DOMAIN"},{"texts":[{"value":"Currently used in vaccines available under the names Daptacel (Sanofi Pasteur), Infanrix (GlaxoSmithKline), Pediarix (GlaxoSmithKline), TriHIBit (Sanofi Pasteur) and Tripedia (Sanofi Pasteur)"}],"commentType":"PHARMACEUTICAL"},{"texts":[{"value":"Thiol:disulfide oxidoreductases DsbA and DsbB are required for periplasmic toxin assembly, whereas DbsC is important for extracellular toxin secretion"}],"commentType":"MISCELLANEOUS"},{"texts":[{"value":"Experiments with temporal expression of PTX indicate that holotoxin secretion is at a rate of 3 molecules/min/cell. Also, more of toxin chains S1, S2 and S3 are produced than secreted; one half of each chain is incorporated into holotoxin"}],"commentType":"MISCELLANEOUS"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305"}],"value":"Belongs to the bacterial exotoxin subunit A family"}],"commentType":"SIMILARITY"}],"features":[{"type":"Signal","location":{"start":{"value":1,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":34,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":""},{"type":"Chain","location":{"start":{"value":35,"m